      \field{abstract}{Despite the attention devoted to phase-field modeling of brittle fracture in the last 20 years, there are still some open issues. Among these, the search for a physically reasonable and computationally feasible criterion to split the energy density into parcels that may or may not contribute to crack propagation has been the subject of many recent studies. Within this context, we propose an energy split -- or energy decomposition -- with the aim of representing as accurately as possible the mechanical behavior of a crack under cyclic loading. To this purpose, two key assumptions are made. The first one is the interpretation of the gradient damage direction as being equivalent to the normal-to-crack direction, as already known by the literature. The second assumption is to consider the sign of the projection of the strain tensor on the normal-to-crack directions at a point as an indicator of whether this point should behave as an open or as a closed crack. We associate the latter case to both (a) a complete recovery of elastic energy density and (b) a zero driving force at such point. The first case is treated classically as a damageable material point at which the crack is free to propagate. Four examples are presented in order to assess the suitability of the present model by using both AT1 and AT2 regularization models. Results show the desired effect of limiting crack propagation to tension as well as of recovering its intact stiffness upon load reversal, even when the two most known energy splits fail to. Finally, the simplicity and effectiveness of our approach may pave the way to more sophisticated idealizations and to complex large-scale applications.}
      \field{journaltitle}{(under revision)}
      \field{title}{A Phase-Field Gradient-Based Energy Split for Modeling of Brittle Fracture under Cyclic Loading}
      \field{year}{2024}
    \endentry
